---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { MapPin, Globe } from 'lucide-astro';

const FRONTMANIA_URL = 'https://frontmania.com/';
const DCND_URL = 'https://www.dutchcloudnativeday.nl/';
const OREDEV_URL = 'https://oredev.org/';
const DDC_URL = 'https://www.developer-world.com/ddc-programm';
const SWISS_URL = 'https://cloudnativeday.ch/sessions/1223005';
const SWISS_SLIDES_URL = 'https://github.com/reverse-gitops/talks/blob/main/dist/gitops-needs-an-api.pdf';
const KUBECON_SCHED_URL = 'https://sched.co/2DY82';
const COZYSUMMIT_URL = 'https://community.cncf.io/events/details/cncf-virtual-project-events-hosted-by-cncf-presents-cozysummit-virtual-2026/';
const COZYSUMMIT_YT_EMBED = 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/dkyjOq-I7Zs';
const COZYSUMMIT_YT_URL = 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dkyjOq-I7Zs';
const COZYSUMMIT_SLIDES_URL = 'https://github.com/reverse-gitops/talks/blob/main/cozystack/slides.pdf';
const KUBECON_YT_EMBED = 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/X3sUAsTPbDM';
const KUBECON_YT_URL = 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X3sUAsTPbDM';
const KUBECON_SLIDES_URL = 'https://github.com/reverse-gitops/talks/blob/main/dist/the-gitops-paradox.pdf';
---

<Layout title="Talks" description="Public talks on Reverse GitOps — the API-first, Git-recorded platform operating model.">
  <main class="page-main">
    <div class="page-content">
      <h1 class="page-title">Talks</h1>

      <section>
        <h2 class="section-label">Upcoming</h2>
        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">Frontmania 2026</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Jaarbeurs, Utrecht · October 7, 2026 · 11:00–11:45 CEST · Mission 2</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">Be BFFs with the Kubernetes API</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Kubernetes is famous for running containers, but its API model is the interesting
            part: flexible resources, desired state, live updates through watches, validation,
            and advanced access control. Platform engineers use these ideas every day.
            Frontenders usually only get the boring REST endpoint at the end. Isn't that a missed
            opportunity?
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            The talk runs an open-source coffee-ordering demo where the whole audience gets admin
            rights and configures it from their phones, showing how live updates are not only
            cool but genuinely useful for handling conflicts. This is not a talk about exposing
            production infrastructure to the public internet. It is about running a small,
            dedicated Kubernetes API server as a product backend — and asking whether Kubernetes
            could become your Backend for Frontend.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={FRONTMANIA_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Frontmania 2026 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">Dutch Cloud Native Day 2026</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Jaarbeurs, Utrecht · October 30, 2026 · 14:40–15:10 CET · Mission 2</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">We GitOps'd Everything Except the Settings That Break Production</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Software teams have a delivery process for code. But what about the runtime
            configuration around that code? It is often self-service for end users, hidden behind
            admin screens and custom database tables, and changed outside the delivery process.
            Feature flags, business rules, and specific domain settings can change production
            behaviour just as much as new code.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            This talk starts with application settings that broke production, and each one leads
            back to the same uncomfortable questions: who made the change, where was it
            validated, and how do we stop the next one? Could important product configuration
            live in a separate Git repo, reviewed and promoted like code, while the people who
            change it never see Git and just use a nice settings screen? The session covers the
            design of an open-source tool built to explore this, where it gets hard (conflicts,
            concurrency, and putting real authors on Git commits), and a live demo with the whole
            audience as admins.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={DCND_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dutch Cloud Native Day 2026 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">Øredev 2026</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Malmö · November 5, 2026 · 14:55–15:35 CET · STAMP</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">Configuration Deserves a Software Delivery Process Too</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Configuration is often some of the most critical logic in a system, yet many teams
            still manage it with far less discipline than code. It lives in databases, gets edited
            through admin screens, bypasses proper review, mixes awkwardly with secrets, and moves
            across environments with limited testing and weak packaging boundaries.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            This talk argues that configuration should be treated as a delivery problem, not just a
            storage problem: capture validated intent through an API, then turn it into
            configuration that is tested, packaged, versioned, signed, and promoted through
            environments as controlled artifacts.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={OREDEV_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Øredev 2026 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">Øredev 2026</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Malmö · November 6, 2026 · 11:15–11:55 CET · TURING</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">(Ab)using the Kubernetes API as a Configuration Backend</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Configuration-heavy products often reinvent the same backend machinery: typed objects,
            validation, permissions, audit trails, and some form of realtime change propagation.
            Over time, that configuration backend starts to look a lot like a control plane.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            This talk explores the Kubernetes API as a backend for configuration-heavy software
            products rather than as an infrastructure surface, including what the approach enables,
            which trade-offs it introduces, and where the model starts to break down.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={OREDEV_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Øredev 2026 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">DDC 2026 · .NET Developer Conference</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Hyatt Regency, Cologne · November 26, 2026 · 09:00–13:00 CET · Half-day workshop</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">What If All Configuration Could Have a Kubernetes-Style API?</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            The best REST API I have ever seen is Kubernetes — not because it runs containers, but
            because the API machinery underneath is flexible enough to model many kinds of intent:
            typed resources, schema validation, optimistic concurrency, watches, status, events,
            audit, authentication, and resource-level authorization. Pods and deployments are just
            the famous examples.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Application configuration is the practical case. This hands-on workshop inspects a
            configurable coffee-ordering app and improves its model together: how to split settings
            across one or more resource types, how to model one-to-many relationships, how to
            define authorization, and the harder question of what happens to existing values when
            the model itself changes. Attendees work in their own devcontainer at two levels —
            first changing the configuration, then changing the definition itself. You do not need
            to be a Kubernetes operator, or even plan to use Kubernetes.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={DDC_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DDC 2026 programme →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="section-label">Past</h2>
        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">Swiss Cloud Native Day 2026</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Mount Gurten, Bern · September 17, 2026</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">GitOps Needs an API</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            GitOps works well — well enough that high-level business intent ends up in Git too:
            customer tenants, feature rollouts, access policies. The hard part is not storing it
            there. The hard part is expecting business stakeholders or customers to manage that
            intent through pull requests and raw YAML.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Reverse GitOps is an API-first pattern for exactly this problem: a typed Kubernetes API
            in front of the repo accepts structured intent through CRDs, without giving callers
            direct access to Git, and a controller turns that intent into YAML files and commits
            them. From there the GitOps workflow you already trust stays intact — review, policy
            checks, CI, and a full audit trail. The talk closed with a live demo of the
            open-source gitops-reverser operator and an honest checklist of where the pattern fits
            and where it does not.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={SWISS_SLIDES_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Slides →</a>
            <a href={SWISS_URL} class="talk-link secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Session page</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">CozySummit Virtual 2026</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <Globe size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Virtual · May 26, 2026 · 17:50–18:20 UTC</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">What If Every Cozystack Change Became a Commit?</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            Cozystack exposes platform services as Kubernetes API resources — Postgres, Redis,
            Bucket, Kubernetes. Users interact in the usual ways: kubectl, the Cozystack
            dashboard, or AI agents via MCP. But who is making all these changes, and why did
            they do it? Git seems far away. But it doesn't have to be.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            What if every platform change automatically appeared as clean YAML in Git? Fully
            automated — a silent layer that turns API activity into a durable, readable history
            of infrastructure intent. Instead of digging through logs or dashboards, teams get
            versioned history they can inspect, diff, and share. The platform continuously writes
            its intent back to Git. Git stops being the interface — and starts being the memory.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-video-wrap">
            <iframe
              src={COZYSUMMIT_YT_EMBED}
              title="What If Every Cozystack Change Became a Commit? — CozySummit Virtual 2026"
              allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
              allowfullscreen
            ></iframe>
          </div>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={COZYSUMMIT_YT_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch on YouTube →</a>
            <a href={COZYSUMMIT_SLIDES_URL} class="talk-link secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Slides</a>
            <a href={COZYSUMMIT_URL} class="talk-link secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Event page</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="talk-card">
          <div class="talk-meta">
            <span class="talk-event">KubeCon + CloudNativeCon Europe 2026 · Platform Engineering Day</span>
            <span class="talk-sep">·</span>
            <MapPin size={13} style="flex-shrink:0;opacity:0.7" />
            <span class="talk-date">Amsterdam · March 23, 2026</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="talk-title">The GitOps Paradox: Why Your Devs Need an API You Don't Want To Build</h3>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            This session argues that GitOps is valuable, but Git does not always need to be the
            human-facing front door for every change. Platform teams often end up rebuilding APIs
            and UIs around workflows that the Kubernetes API already models well. Reverse GitOps
            explores a different approach: use a small, typed Kubernetes control plane for user
            intent, then write that intent back into Git so review, promotion, and auditability
            stay intact.
          </p>
          <p class="talk-desc">
            The talk includes two live demos: a simple application writing user intent into the
            Kubernetes API and syncing it back to Git; and a higher-level platform resource
            flowing from API input to Git to preview and production deployment.
          </p>
          <div class="talk-video-wrap">
            <iframe
              src={KUBECON_YT_EMBED}
              title="The GitOps Paradox — KubeCon Platform Engineering Day 2026"
              allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
              allowfullscreen
            ></iframe>
          </div>
          <div class="talk-links">
            <a href={KUBECON_YT_URL} class="talk-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch on YouTube →</a>
            <a href={KUBECON_SLIDES_URL} class="talk-link secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Slides</a>
            <a href={KUBECON_SCHED_URL} class="talk-link secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Session page</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
